- Past CV history: hypertension, heart failure, coronary artery disease, prior stroke/TIA, peripheral vascular disease.
- Depression meds (SSRIs/SNRIs) — these can modestly raise bleeding risk when combined with anticoagulants.

How clinicians assess stroke and bleeding risk (useful to discuss)
- CHA2DS2-VASc stroke-risk score: assigns points for Congestive heart failure, Hypertension, Age ≥75 (2 points), Diabetes (1), Stroke/TIA prior (2), Vascular disease, Age 65–74 (1), Sex female (1). If you have diabetes (you said type 2), that’s at least 1 point — anticoagulation recommendations depend on the total score. Ask your PCP to calculate this with your exact details.
- HAS-BLED bleeding-risk score: looks at Hypertension, Abnormal renal/liver function, Stroke history, Bleeding history or predisposition, Labile INRs (if on warfarin), Elderly (age >65), Drugs or alcohol that increase bleeding risk. This helps guide monitoring and risk mitigation, not necessarily to avoid anticoagulation.

Anticoagulation considerations to discuss
- If CHA2DS2-VASc indicates benefit from anticoagulation, ask about DOACs (apixaban, rivaroxaban, dabigatran, edoxaban) vs warfarin. DOACs are commonly preferred for non-valvular AF but choice depends on kidney function, drug interactions, cost/coverage, and other health issues.
- Kidney function matters a lot: several DOACs require dose reduction or avoidance at lower creatinine clearance. Confirm your creatinine/eGFR so the right drug and dose can be chosen.
- Interactions: many of your meds won’t interact heavily, but SSRIs/SNRIs and antiplatelet drugs increase bleeding risk when combined with anticoagulants. Proton pump inhibitors (omeprazole) are usually safe with DOACs; they can reduce dabigatran absorption somewhat but are often continued if indicated.
- If you’re already on an anticoagulant, ask whether the dose is correct for your kidney function and weight, and whether any drug interactions apply.

When to consider cardiology referral (or electrophysiology)
You do not automatically need a cardiology referral because of age alone. Discuss referral if any of the following apply:
- New diagnosis of AF — many PCPs will refer or co-manage depending on local practice.
- Recurrent or symptomatic AF despite initial therapy (palpitations limiting life, syncope).
- Difficult-to-manage rate or rhythm control (unable to control heart rate, recurrent symptomatic AF despite meds).
- Need for advanced options: consideration of catheter ablation, left atrial appendage occlusion (Watchman) for stroke prevention, or complex anticoagulation decisions.
- Significant structural heart disease suspected (valvular disease, cardiomyopathy) or abnormal echocardiogram.
- High stroke risk where anticoagulation strategy is complex (e.g., severe renal impairment) or prior bleeding on anticoagulant.
- If your PCP feels you need specialized input or you want a second opinion on rhythm-control strategies.

Recommended tests and checks to request
- 12-lead ECG (if not done recently) — confirms AF and rhythm.
- Ambulatory rhythm monitoring (Holter or event monitor) if symptoms are intermittent or to quantify burden.
- Transthoracic echocardiogram — check for structural heart disease, left atrial size, ventricular function, valvular disease.
- Labs: creatinine, eGFR, electrolytes, liver tests, A1c (for diabetes control), thyroid function (hyperthyroidism can cause AF), CBC (anemia), and possibly BNP if heart failure suspected.
- If anticoagulation is started: baseline renal and liver labs, and periodic monitoring afterward.

Practical questions to ask Dr. Wilson (or to bring up)
- Do I have AF (or is it suspected)? If yes, what type and how is it being managed now?
- Based on my diabetes and other factors, what is my CHA2DS2-VASc score and do you recommend anticoagulation?
- What are the pros/cons of starting a DOAC vs warfarin for me, given my kidney function and current meds?
- Should I see Cardiology (or Electrophysiology/Colorado Cardiology)? Is a referral appropriate now or only if I remain symptomatic or treatments fail?
- What tests should we order now (ECG, Holter, echo, labs)?
- Are there lifestyle changes or symptom triggers I should avoid? (e.g., limit alcohol/caffeine, treat sleep apnea if present)
- If you recommend anticoagulation, how will we monitor kidney function and bleeding risk over time?
- Are any of my current meds or supplements cause for concern with anticoagulation?

Red flags that need urgent care or immediate evaluation
- New neurologic symptoms (speech, weakness, vision loss) — call emergency services (possible stroke).
- Chest pain, severe shortness of breath, fainting — seek urgent evaluation.

If you want, I can:
- Draft a concise message/email for Dr. Wilson summarizing your concerns and asking about referral to Colorado Cardiology and the tests above (I can prepare it for you to send).  
- Walk through a sample CHA2DS2-VASc/HAS-BLED calculation if you give me sex and confirm other comorbidities.  
- Help prepare a one-page checklist you can bring to the appointment.

Which of those would you like next? And please confirm the creatinine value, sex, whether you already have an AF diagnosis, and your current medications (including any blood thinners or antidepressants).

