Below are things to think through and to take to your annual physical with Dr. James Wilson, grouped by topic.

Immediate/important
- Clarify your kidney function lab (see Q1 above). Kidney function strongly affects anticoagulant choices and dosing. If your creatinine/eGFR is markedly abnormal, that changes management and may warrant more urgent cardiology involvement.

Stroke risk and bleeding risk — how they guide anticoagulation
- CHA2DS2‑VASc estimates stroke risk in patients with AF. Points: Congestive HF (1), Hypertension (1), Age ≥75 (2), Diabetes (1), Stroke/TIA/thromboembolism (2), Vascular disease (1), Age 65–74 (1), Female sex (1). Your diabetes gives you at least 1 point. If you tell me your sex and other conditions, I can calculate this for you.
   - Typical guidance: men with score ≥2 and women with score ≥3 are recommended oral anticoagulation; men with score 1 (women score 2 when only female sex counts) are a gray zone and need shared decision-making.
- HAS‑BLED estimates bleeding risk (Hypertension, Abnormal LFT/renal, Stroke history, Bleeding history, Labile INR, Elderly >65, Drugs/alcohol). A high HAS‑BLED score suggests caution and modification of risk factors (control BP, avoid interacting drugs, correct anemia) but is not an automatic contraindication to anticoagulation.

Tests and data you should bring or request
- Recent labs: BMP (creatinine, electrolytes), CBC, LFTs, A1c, TSH. Bring the exact creatinine and eGFR.
- Any prior ECGs, Holter/event monitor reports, echocardiogram reports if you have them.
