4) How your age and diabetes affect decisions
- Age 55 alone does not automatically require anticoagulation; diabetes gives 1 point on CHA2DS2-VASc. If you’re male and only have diabetes (score 1), anticoagulation is a discussion (some clinicians recommend starting it, others consider watchful waiting depending on additional factors and patient preference). If you have other risk factors (HTN, prior stroke, vascular disease) your score will be higher and anticoagulation is more clearly indicated.
- Kidney function is key: many DOACs require dose adjustment or aren’t recommended below certain eGFR thresholds. That makes knowing your true creatinine/eGFR essential.

5) When to consider referral to cardiology (Colorado Cardiology or similar)
Recommend cardiology referral if any of the following apply:
- New diagnosis of AF (to define type and investigate causes).
- Symptomatic AF affecting quality of life (frequent palpitations, syncope, presyncope, limiting exercise).
