- Recurrent or difficult-to-control AF despite meds and you’re considering rhythm control strategies (antiarrhythmics or catheter ablation).
- Structural heart disease on echo (valvular disease, reduced EF).
- Complex anticoagulation decisions (e.g., severe kidney disease, prior intracranial bleed, high bleeding risk) or need for left atrial appendage closure discussion.
- Planning an invasive procedure where AF management is complex.

If none of those apply and your AF (if present) is well-controlled, asymptomatic, and anticoagulation decisions are straightforward, your PCP can often manage this with periodic cardiology input as needed.

