Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
I can help — first a couple of quick clarifying questions, then practical guidance and suggested questions/scripts you can use with Dr. James Wilson.

Quick clarifications
- Do you already have a diagnosis of atrial fibrillation, or are you asking about AF prevention/monitoring because of risk factors or symptoms (palpitations, lightheadedness, shortness of breath, fainting)?
- When you say “last creatinine was 28,” do you mean serum creatinine 2.8 mg/dL, creatinine 28 µmol/L, or eGFR = 28 mL/min/1.73m2? (28 mg/dL would be incompatible with life—so I want to be sure which value it is.)
- What sex do you identify as? (CHA2DS2-VASc stroke-risk scoring uses sex.)
- Any other medical history I should know about: hypertension, prior stroke or TIA, coronary artery disease, heart failure, bleeding history, liver disease, current anticoagulants or antiplatelets, current antidepressant(s)?

Overview — what matters for AF management and referral
- Stroke risk: If you have AF, stroke risk is assessed with CHA2DS2-VASc. Age 55 alone does not add points; diabetes gives 1 point. For men, a score of 1 is a borderline indication to “consider” anticoagulation; for women the thresholds differ slightly because female sex adds 1 point.
- Kidney function matters a lot. Reduced kidney function (e.g., eGFR ≤30 mL/min) changes which anticoagulants are safe and dosing. If your renal function is impaired, that increases both thrombotic and bleeding risks and is an important reason to involve a specialist when choosing anticoagulation.
- Bleeding risk: HAS-BLED estimates bleeding risk and identifies modifiable factors (uncontrolled HTN, renal/liver disease, drugs/alcohol). A higher bleeding risk doesn’t automatically rule out anticoagulation — it means you and your clinician should address modifiable risks and choose the safest anticoagulant/dose.
- Rate vs rhythm control: Many patients with AF are managed by PCPs for rate control and anticoagulation, but rhythm-control strategies (antiarrhythmic drugs, cardioversion, ablation) and complex cases (heart failure, structural heart disease) usually need cardiology input.
- Monitoring: New AF or intermittent symptoms often prompt ECG, ambulatory monitoring (Holter or event monitor), and echocardiogram to assess heart structure.

When to consider referral to cardiology (Colorado Cardiology)
- New diagnosis of atrial fibrillation (especially if symptomatic)
- Recurrent or symptomatic palpitations, syncope, presyncope, or heart-failure symptoms that might be AF-related
- Unclear stroke-risk anticoagulation decision because of CKD or complex comorbidities
- Difficulty controlling heart rate or rhythm with medications
- Considering antiarrhythmic drugs, cardioversion, or AF ablation
- Structural heart disease, reduced ejection fraction, or prior thromboembolic event despite therapy
- Multiple drug interactions or complicated anticoagulation needs (e.g., on multiple interacting meds, low/high body weight, extremes of renal function)

Practical questions to bring to Dr. Wilson
- Do I currently have atrial fibrillation (or any arrhythmia) on file? If not, do you recommend screening (ECG, pulse checks, or ambulatory monitor) given my age and diabetes?
- Can we review my most recent kidney function results (serum creatinine and eGFR)? How does this affect anticoagulant choices if AF is present?
- If I had AF, what would my stroke risk be (CHA2DS2-VASc) and would you recommend anticoagulation?
- If anticoagulation is recommended, which agent would you prefer given my kidney function and current meds (omeprazole, gabapentin, and any antidepressant)? Will dose adjustments or monitoring be needed?
- Do any of my current medications (including antidepressants) increase bleeding risk with an anticoagulant?
- Do you think I should see a cardiologist (Colorado Cardiology) now, or only if AF is confirmed or if I become symptomatic? If a referral is recommended, can you send one?
- If I already have AF: Are we aiming for rate or rhythm control? Do I need an echocardiogram, Holter, or event monitor? When should I come back for follow-up?
- Are there modifiable bleeding-risk factors we should address (blood pressure control, alcohol, other meds)?
- If anticoagulation is started, how will we follow renal function and adjust dosing over time?

Notes on medication interactions and specifics
- DOACs (apixaban, rivaroxaban, dabigatran, edoxaban) are preferred over aspirin for stroke prevention in many patients with AF, but dosing depends on kidney function. Severe renal impairment often requires specialist input.
- Warfarin may be preferred in severe CKD (or dialysis) in some cases, but it requires INR monitoring and has its own tradeoffs.
- Proton pump inhibitors (omeprazole) reduce upper GI bleeding risk but may interact with some drugs; overall PPIs are often continued when patients are on anticoagulants if there is GI bleed risk.
- Some antidepressants (SSRIs/SNRIs) modestly increase bleeding risk when combined with anticoagulants — important to discuss with your PCP.
- Gabapentin has few direct interactions with anticoagulants, but always review the full med list.

