- Antidepressants (especially SSRIs, SNRIs) can increase bleeding risk slightly when combined with anticoagulants — tell your PCP what antidepressant(s) you take.  
- If you are or may be on antiplatelet therapy (aspirin, clopidogrel), discuss combined bleeding risk.

Practical items to bring/prepare
- Complete and current medication list with doses (prescription, OTCs, supplements).  
- Recent lab results (or request them before visit).  
- A written list of symptoms (palpitations, SOB, exercise tolerance, chest pain, dizziness, falls).  
- Any rhythm recordings (smartwatch, event monitor).  
- A clear question about whether you want PCP to refer you to Colorado Cardiology or electrophysiology for further evaluation.

When to seek a cardiology referral now
- If AF is newly diagnosed and you want specialist input on rhythm management or anticoagulation choices given other medical issues.  
- If you have symptomatic AF despite initial therapy, structural heart disease, prior stroke, or complex renal dosing issues.  
- If you want to discuss catheter ablation as a strategy.

