I can help — this is a good set of things to prepare before your annual physical. A few quick clarifying questions first (they matter for anticoagulation and referral decisions):

- Do you already have a diagnosis of atrial fibrillation (AF)? If so, when was it diagnosed and what kind (paroxysmal, persistent, permanent)?  
- Are you currently taking any blood thinner / anticoagulant (warfarin, apixaban, rivaroxaban, dabigatran, edoxaban)?  
- Do you have a history of stroke or TIA, heart failure, hypertension, vascular disease (prior heart attack / PAD), or major bleeding?  
- What sex do you identify as (male / female)? (CHA2DS2‑VASc uses sex for scoring.)  
- Can you confirm the recent creatinine value and its units (e.g., mg/dL or µmol/L)? A value of “28” could mean 28 µmol/L (very low) or 28 mg/dL (incompatible with life); I need the units to interpret kidney function. Also give your approximate weight if you know it.  
- Is gabapentin for neuropathic pain? (You listed it with GERD; gabapentin is not a GERD medication.)  
- Any current symptoms from AF (palpitations, shortness of breath, lightheadedness, syncope, exercise intolerance)?

What matters clinically (brief overview)
- Stroke risk assessment: AF stroke prevention is guided by CHA2DS2‑VASc. Diabetes = 1 point. Age 55 does not give points (age points start at 65). If your overall CHA2DS2‑VASc is:
  - 0 (men) — usually no anticoagulation,
  - 1 (men) — anticoagulation may be considered based on shared decision-making,
  - ≥2 (men) — anticoagulation is generally recommended.
  (For women add +1 for female sex when calculating thresholds.)
- Bleeding risk and renal function: Bleeding risk (HAS‑BLED) matters but does not automatically rule out anticoagulation; it informs monitoring and modifiable risk reduction. Accurate renal function (creatinine/eGFR or creatinine clearance) is essential because DOAC dosing and even choice (DOAC vs warfarin) depends on kidney function. If CrCl is very low (for example <15 mL/min or dialysis), DOACs may be contraindicated and warfarin or nephrology guidance may be needed.
- Rhythm vs rate control: Choice depends on symptoms, duration of AF, heart structure/function, and patient preference. Symptomatic patients or those with heart failure/ventricular dysfunction may need cardiology input for rhythm control strategies (antiarrhythmic drugs, cardioversion, or ablation).
- When to refer to cardiology (good triggers)
  - New diagnosis of AF and you/PCP want cardiology input on anticoagulation or rhythm strategy
  - Symptomatic AF (palpitations limiting activity, syncope, severe dyspnea)
  - Uncontrolled heart rate despite therapy or difficult-to-manage symptoms
  - Recurrent or persistent AF being considered for cardioversion or ablation
  - Prior stroke/TIA while in AF, or complex anticoagulation decisions (recent bleeding, very low or very high CrCl, multiple interacting meds)
  - Evidence of structural heart disease, significant valve disease, or reduced left ventricular ejection fraction
  - Need for specialized testing (EPS, advanced imaging)
- Age and diabetes alone (age 55 + diabetes) are not automatic reasons to refer to cardiology. Many patients with low symptom burden and straightforward anticoagulation/rate control can be managed by a PCP with appropriate testing and follow‑up. But if any of the triggers above apply, a cardiology referral is reasonable.

Tests your PCP may order before deciding on referral
- 12‑lead ECG
- Basic metabolic panel (creatinine, electrolytes) and eGFR
- CBC (to check baseline hemoglobin/platelets)
- TSH (thyroid dysfunction can cause AF)
- Echocardiogram (assess structure, valves, EF)
- Holter monitor or event monitor if suspected intermittent AF
- Lipid panel, HbA1c (diabetes control), BP check

Suggested questions to ask Dr. James Wilson at your visit
- “Do I have atrial fibrillation (or how was AF classified) and what is my current stroke risk (CHA2DS2‑VASc)?”
- “Do you recommend starting or continuing an anticoagulant for stroke prevention? If so, which one and at what dose — and how will we monitor renal function for dosing?”
- “Is my kidney function adequate for a DOAC? Can you confirm my creatinine/eGFR and my current weight for dosing?”
- “Am I having AF symptoms that need cardiology evaluation for rhythm control or procedures (cardioversion/ablation)?”
- “Do I need an echocardiogram, Holter, or other testing now?”
- “Given my age (55) and diabetes, do you recommend a referral to cardiology (Colorado Cardiology), or can you manage this here?”
- “Are there any medication interactions or bleeding risks we should address (e.g., with my other meds or history of falls/bleeding)?”

Practical checklist to bring to your appointment
- List of current medications, doses, and what each is for (include OTCs and supplements)
- Recent lab values (especially creatinine with units) and recent HbA1c, lipids, BP readings
- Any ECGs, cardiology notes, or hospitalization records for AF
- A short symptom diary (palpitations, dizziness, shortness of breath, episodes and duration)
- Questions above written out

If you’d like, I can:
- Draft a short message/email to Dr. Wilson asking whether he recommends referral to Colorado Cardiology and listing key items to check (I can draft it now).  
- Help you calculate a CHA2DS2‑VASc score if you tell me your sex and whether you have hypertension, prior stroke/TIA, vascular disease, or heart failure.  
- Interpret/clarify your creatinine if you can confirm the units and give your weight.

Tell me the answers to the clarifying questions you can, and whether you want a draft message to your PCP requesting cardiology referral or specific tests.

