I can help — this is a good time to prepare focused questions. A few quick clarifying questions first, then practical talking points, tests to have ready, and guidance on when referral to cardiology is appropriate.

Clarifying questions
- Do you already have a diagnosis of atrial fibrillation (AF)? If so, when was it diagnosed and what type (paroxysmal, persistent, permanent)?
- Are you currently on any blood thinners (warfarin, apixaban, rivaroxaban, dabigatran, edoxaban)? Any prior strokes or TIAs?
- Do you have high blood pressure, heart failure, prior heart attack, or vascular disease?
- Do you have symptoms from the AF (palpitations, shortness of breath, lightheadedness, fainting)?
- Can you confirm your current medication list and doses? (You listed omeprazole 20 mg and gabapentin 300 mg — omeprazole is typically for GERD; gabapentin is usually for neuropathic pain or mood/neurologic symptoms. Also tell me any diabetes meds.)
- About your creatinine: you wrote “last creatinine was 28.” What units were used (mg/dL or µmol/L)? That number is important to interpret kidney function and anticoagulant dosing.

Important immediate items / red flags
- Seek urgent care or call your PCP/cardiology if you have chest pain, new or worsening shortness of breath, fainting/syncope, or stroke symptoms (sudden weakness, slurred speech, vision loss, confusion).
- If your creatinine is 28 mg/dL (extremely high), that’s a medical emergency. If it’s 28 µmol/L (very low), that’s different — please confirm units.

Key points about AF management relevant to your age and diabetes
- Stroke risk: CHA2DS2‑VASc is the usual tool. For men: 1 point for diabetes; age 55 does not score. A score of 1 (from diabetes alone) typically requires an individualized discussion — many clinicians will recommend oral anticoagulation after weighing bleeding risk and preferences.
- Bleeding risk: HAS‑BLED helps assess bleeding risk; kidney function and other meds matter. Impaired renal function can change the choice and dosing of anticoagulants (some DOACs require dose adjustments or are contraindicated at low eGFR).
- Rate vs rhythm control: For many patients, rate control (beta‑blocker, nondihydropyridine calcium channel blocker, or digoxin in some cases) is enough. Rhythm control (antiarrhythmic drugs or catheter ablation) is considered if symptoms persist or for younger patients where maintaining sinus rhythm is prioritized.
- Diabetes increases stroke risk, so anticoagulation conversation is important even at age 55.
- Kidney function and drug interactions: eGFR is required to choose/dose DOACs. Also tell your clinician about all meds — PPIs (omeprazole) and some other drugs can interact with antiplatelets/anticoagulants or affect absorption/metabolism of other meds. Gabapentin has fewer interactions but is sedating in some people.

Tests and information to have ready for the appointment
- Recent labs: creatinine and eGFR, electrolytes, liver function tests, CBC, HbA1c, lipid panel. If on warfarin: most recent INR.
- Recent ECG; if none, ask for a 12‑lead ECG.
- Any ambulatory monitoring results (Holter, event monitor) or notes about episodes/timing/symptoms.
- Current med list (including OTCs, herbals) and allergies.
- Blood pressure readings over the last weeks, weight, and history of falls/bleeding.

Questions to ask Dr. James Wilson (PCP) about AF and need for cardiology referral
- Based on my risk factors (diabetes and age 55), do you recommend anticoagulation now? Which agent and why?
- Do you think my kidney function is adequate for DOAC therapy, or would warfarin be safer?
- Should I have an echocardiogram, longer ambulatory monitor (e.g., 14–30 day patch), or other imaging before deciding on therapy?
- Are my symptoms or AF pattern such that I should see a cardiologist/electrophysiologist (Colorado Cardiology) for rhythm management or ablation?
- Do any of my current medications or conditions change the anticoagulation choice or dose?
- Do I need urgent referral or can this be managed in primary care with cardiology input as needed?

When referral to cardiology (or electrophysiology) is usually recommended
- New diagnosis of AF with uncertainty about anticoagulation or complex anticoagulation decisions (e.g., severe CKD, prior major bleeding).
- Symptomatic AF despite rate control (palpitations, dizziness, dyspnea affecting quality of life).
- Consideration of rhythm-control strategies (antiarrhythmic drugs or catheter ablation), especially if you prefer rhythm control or are younger.
- Recurrent or difficult-to-control arrhythmia, syncope, or structural heart disease on echo.
- If you want a second opinion about anticoagulation given your diabetes and other risks.

Suggested script if you want to ask Dr. Wilson for a referral to Colorado Cardiology
“Dr. Wilson — I have type 2 diabetes and we’ve identified atrial fibrillation (or I’m worried about AF). I’d like cardiology input about whether I should start anticoagulation and whether I’m a candidate for rhythm-control strategies. Given my risk factors and kidney function, can you refer me to Colorado Cardiology (or an electrophysiologist) for evaluation, or do you prefer to manage this here?”

What I can help you prepare next
- If you want, I can draft a concise note or message to Dr. Wilson requesting a referral or specific tests.
- If you confirm: (1) whether you have a formal AF diagnosis, (2) current anticoagulant status, (3) your other comorbidities (HTN, CHF, stroke history), and (4) the units for that creatinine value — I can give more specific, personalized talking points and an estimated CHA2DS2‑VASc/HAS‑BLED interpretation.

